## SECTION 2 — LESSON IDENTIFICATION TABLE
<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <tr>
        <td style="width: 50%;"><b>Lesson #:</b> 2</td>
        <td style="width: 50%;"><b>Skills Focus:</b> Reading</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
        <td><b>Grade:</b> 5th Grade (CEFR: A1.3)</td>
        <td><b>Scenario:</b> Time for Exercise</td>
        <td><b>Theme:</b> We Can Swim.</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
        <td><b>Date(s):</b> From ___ to ___</td>
        <td colspan="2"><b>Learning Sequence Time:</b> 45–50 min</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
        <td colspan="3">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Specific Objective:</b> Reading: Respond to texts with personal reflections. Reading: Participate in group discussions based on reading assignments. Phonemic Awareness: Decode English graphemes and phonemes using knowledge of word parts, syllabification, and phonemic awareness.</p>
        </td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
        <td colspan="3">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Learning Outcome:</b> Reading: Can respond to texts about exercise, with personal reflections (e.g., "I think exercising is fun."). Can participate in group discussions about exercise, based on reading assignments (e.g., "What are some simple exercises we can do?" "We can jump rope, ride a bike, or run."). Can decode English graphemes and phonemes by segmenting words into parts, identifying syllables, and applying phonemic awareness (e.g., decoding "wellness" as "well-ness" and "exciting" as "ex-cit-ing").</p>
        </td>
    </tr>
</table>

## SECTION 3 — AOA STAGES TABLE
<h2 style="color: #1a5276; text-align: center;">The Six Action-Oriented Approach (AOA) Lesson Stages</h2>
<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <thead>
        <tr>
            <th style="width: 33.3%; background-color: #1a5276; color: white;">Stage & Description</th>
            <th style="width: 33.3%; background-color: #1a5276; color: white;">Activities & Teacher Actions</th>
            <th style="width: 33.3%; background-color: #1a5276; color: white;">Estimated Date and Time</th>
        </tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
        <tr>
            <td class="aoa-stage-title" colspan="3">STAGE 1 — Warm-up / Pre-task (Engagement, Modeling and Clarification)</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">This stage focuses on engaging students, activating prior knowledge, and introducing key language through modeling and clarification before deeper engagement with the reading task.</p>
            </td>
            <td>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Warm-up:</b> The teacher displays flashcards or pictures of different exercises and sports: <i>swimming, running, cycling, dancing, football, hiking</i>. Teacher points to each picture and asks, "What is this? Is it an exercise? Do you like it?" Students respond chorally or individually (e.g., "It's swimming!", "Yes, it is!", "I like swimming.").</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Modeling:</b> The teacher models sentences related to the warm-up activities, focusing on target grammar and pronunciation. Teacher says, "I like swimming. I <b>can</b> <b>swim</b>." (emphasizing /k/ and /s/). "My friend <b>can</b> run. Running is healthy." "I like <b>dancing</b>. It's <b>fun</b>!"</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Clarification (CCQs):</b> The teacher checks understanding of the vocabulary and concepts.
                        <ul>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">CCQ 1: "Is swimming a sport?" (Expected answer: Yes!)</li>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">CCQ 2: "Is it good for you to exercise?" (Expected answer: Yes!)</li>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">CCQ 3: "Can a cat swim?" (Expected answer: No!)</li>
                        </ul>
                    </li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td><p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">5-7 min</p></td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td class="aoa-stage-title" colspan="3">STAGE 2 — Presentation</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Students are presented with a short, authentic-like text related to the scenario "Time for Exercise" to introduce new language in context and reinforce previously learned vocabulary.</p>
            </td>
            <td>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Material:</b> A short, illustrated article titled "Fun Ways to Exercise!" presented on a projector or as a handout. The text describes a few simple exercises and highlights their benefits using target vocabulary and grammar.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Communicative Competence:</b> Reads and comprehends short, simple informational texts about daily routines and leisure activities, identifying key information and author's purpose.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Text Example:</b>
                        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Fun Ways to Exercise!</b></p>
                        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Hi friends! Do you like sports? I always like to be <b>active</b>! Every day, after school in the <b>afternoon</b>, I do some <b>exercise</b>. What type of exercise do you like? I like <b>swimming</b>! I <b>can swim</b> very well. My <b>friend</b> Ana likes <b>cycling</b>. We sometimes go to the park and <b>ride</b> our bikes. My brother likes <b>running</b> and playing <b>football</b>. He <b>can</b> run fast! Exercise makes us <b>healthy</b> and <b>fit</b>. It’s so <b>exciting</b>!</p>
                    </li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Comprehension tasks (Gist questions):</b>
                        <ul>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">The teacher asks, "What is the text about?" (Expected: About exercise/sports).</li>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">The teacher asks, "Who likes swimming?" (Expected: The writer/narrator).</li>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">The teacher asks, "What do friends do sometimes?" (Expected: Go cycling/ride bikes).</li>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Students read the text silently first, then the teacher reads it aloud, modeling pronunciation.</li>
                        </ul>
                    </li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td><p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">10-12 min</p></td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td class="aoa-stage-title" colspan="3">STAGE 3 — Preparation / Practice</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">This stage focuses on controlled and semi-controlled reading practice to build accuracy in identifying and using target grammar, vocabulary, and phonemic awareness from the text.</p>
            </td>
            <td>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Communicative Skill & Content:</b> Reading comprehension, identifying modal verbs (can, cannot), present simple questions and affirmative sentences, WH-questions + -ing. Vocabulary: <i>walk, exercise, afternoon, gym, sport, activity, friend, football, swimming, running, hiking, dancing, cycling, healthy, active, fit, fun, exciting, every day, always, sometimes</i>. Pronunciation: /k/ in "can" and /s/ in "swim", segmenting multisyllabic words (e.g., "wellness", "exciting").</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Detailed Procedure:</b>
                        <ul>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>1. Controlled Practice (Gap-fill):</b> The teacher provides a worksheet with sentences from the "Fun Ways to Exercise!" text, but with key target words (verbs, nouns, modals, adjectives, adverbs) missing. Students read the sentences and fill in the blanks using a word bank or by recalling from the text.
                                <ul>
                                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Example: "I ____ swim very well." (can)</li>
                                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Example: "My friend Ana likes ____." (cycling)</li>
                                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Example: "Exercise makes us ____ and fit." (healthy)</li>
                                </ul>
                            </li>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>2. Semi-controlled Practice (Sentence Completion & True/False):</b>
                                <ul>
                                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Students complete sentences based on the text or their own ideas, using specific grammatical structures. Example: "What type of exercise do you like? I like _____." (e.g., swimming, running, dancing).</li>
                                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Teacher provides True/False statements about the text. Students read each statement and circle T or F, justifying their answer by pointing to the relevant part of the text.
                                        <ul>
                                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Statement: "The writer likes football." (False - brother likes football)</li>
                                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Statement: "Ana and her friend sometimes ride bikes." (True)</li>
                                        </ul>
                                    </li>
                                </ul>
                            </li>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>3. Phonemic Awareness & Pair Check:</b>
                                <ul>
                                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">The teacher writes multisyllabic words from the text on the board (e.g., "wellness," "exciting," "afternoon," "activity"). The teacher models segmenting them into syllables (e.g., "well-ness," "ex-cit-ing"). Students then identify other multisyllabic words in the text and practice segmenting them in pairs.</li>
                                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Students work in pairs to re-read parts of the text, taking turns. They ask each other "Do you like to swim?" or "What type of exercise do you like?" based on the text and personal preferences, expressing "I like swimming."</li>
                                </ul>
                            </li>
                        </ul>
                    </li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td><p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">15-18 min</p></td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td class="aoa-stage-title" colspan="3">STAGE 4 — Performance / Production</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Students apply their reading comprehension and language skills in a collaborative, realistic scenario, discussing personal reflections and encouraging peers.</p>
            </td>
            <td>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Task Format:</b> Group Discussion - "Our Exercise Ideas!"</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Procedure:</b>
                        <ul>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Students are divided into small groups (3-4 students).</li>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Each group rereads the "Fun Ways to Exercise!" text and discusses what exercises they liked from the text and why.</li>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">They then brainstorm and discuss other simple exercises they can do in the afternoon or every day.</li>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Students use the target grammar: "What type of exercise do you like?" "I like swimming." "My friend can play football." They also practice encouraging others: "Let's run together!" "You can do it!"</li>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Each group prepares to share 2-3 favorite exercises and explain why they like them, or suggest a group activity.</li>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Teacher monitors groups, providing support and noting language use.</li>
                        </ul>
                    </li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td><p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">10-12 min</p></td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td class="aoa-stage-title" colspan="3">STAGE 5 — Assessment / Post-task</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Formative assessment measures students' ability to respond to texts, participate in discussions, and use target language and phonemic awareness, followed by self-reflection.</p>
            </td>
            <td>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Formative Assessment Strategy:</b> Teacher observation checklist during group discussions. The checklist includes criteria for participation, use of target vocabulary, grammar, and pronunciation. Peer assessment can be included where students briefly evaluate their group members on participation.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Assessment Task:</b> Students participate actively in the group discussion, sharing their personal reflections about exercising based on the reading, and proposing activities. Each group shares their findings with the class.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Evaluation Criteria:</b>
                        <ul>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Intelligible pronunciation of /k/ in "can" and /s/ in "swim", and clear segmenting of multisyllabic words.</li>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Use of target vocabulary (e.g., <i>swimming, running, healthy, fun, exciting, friend</i>).</li>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Correct use of modal verbs (can/cannot) and present simple questions/affirmative sentences.</li>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Clarity of ideas when responding to the text and expressing personal preferences.</li>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Collaboration and encouraging others within the group (e.g., "Let's run together!").</li>
                        </ul>
                    </li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Student Self-Assessment / Exit Ticket:</b> Students complete an exit ticket with the following prompts:
                        <ul>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">1. "Today I learned new words about _______."</li>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">2. "I can read about exercises and say what I ______." (like/think)</li>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">3. "Was it easy to work with my friends? Yes / No / A little."</li>
                        </ul>
                    </li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td><p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">5 min</p></td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td class="aoa-stage-title" colspan="3">STAGE 6 — Reflection</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">This final stage encourages self-reflection for students and critical reflection for the teacher, linking the current lesson to future learning objectives.</p>
            </td>
            <td>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Student Self-Reflection Prompt:</b>
                        <ul>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What new words about exercise did I learn or practice today from the reading?"</li>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Can I tell a friend what my favorite exercise is and why I like it?"</li>
                        </ul>
                    </li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Teacher Reflection:</b>
                        <ul>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What worked well in this reading lesson? (e.g., student engagement with the text, participation in discussions, clarity of instructions)."</li>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What adjustments are needed for future reading lessons or for the next lesson in the sequence? (e.g., more visual aids for decoding, additional support for forming personal reflections, more guided practice for specific grammar points)."</li>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Were the phonemic awareness activities effective for decoding multisyllabic words?"</li>
                        </ul>
                    </li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Connection to next lesson in the theme sequence:</b> The reading comprehension and group discussions from this lesson will serve as a strong foundation for Lesson 3, which focuses on Speaking & Interaction. Students will use the same vocabulary and grammar to engage in more structured spoken activities, such as role-playing a conversation about inviting a friend to exercise.</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td><p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">5 min</p></td>
        </tr>
    </tbody>
</table>